---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Btn from '../../components/Btn.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { TOOLS } from '../../data/tools';

const groups = [...new Set(TOOLS.map(t => t.category))];
---
<Base title="Seller software pricing" description="What the main Amazon seller tools actually cost in 2026, with every figure sourced and dated — and the disagreements between published sources recorded rather than resolved.">
<div class="wrap"><Breadcrumbs trail={[{ label: 'Tools', href: '/reviews/' }]} /></div>

<section class="wrap" style="padding:1.5rem 1.25rem 0">
  <p class="eyebrow">Software</p>
  <h1 style="max-width:20ch">What seller software actually costs</h1>
  <p style="max-width:46ch;font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem">
    Nine tools, priced from published sources with the date each figure was checked. Half of them
    carry a contested block, because sources dated within months of each other give different
    numbers for the same plan — usually monthly against annual, sometimes a retired plan that
    directories never removed.
  </p>

  <p class="note" style="margin:0 0 2rem">
    <strong>These are pricing references, not tested reviews.</strong> No tool here has been
    purchased or used by this site. Scored reviews follow the <a href="/how-we-test/">published
    rubric</a>, are bought at list price, and state the plan tier and month tested. Some outbound
    links are commissioned — see the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>Reported entry price</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {TOOLS.map(t => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong><a href={`/reviews/${t.slug}/`}>{t.name}</a></strong><br /><span class="note">{t.oneLiner}</span></td>
            <td>{t.category}</td>
            <td>{t.pricing[0].v}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <AdSlot inline={true} label="Software index — mid page" />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why the prices disagree</h2>
    <p>
      Three causes account for nearly all of it. Vendors quote monthly and annual rates from the same
      headline, and a source that does not state the billing term is unusable. Promotions run and
      lapse without the published figure being revisited. And software directories carry prices they
      have not rechecked in years — one listing in this set gives a figure whose own disclaimer dates
      it to 2019.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical rule is the same one that applies to fee schedules elsewhere on this site: a price
      without a date is a rumour with a decimal point. Every figure here carries the date it was
      checked, and the vendor's own page is the only current source.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you subscribe to anything</h2>
    <p>
      Software is a small line in most seller cost structures and it attracts disproportionate
      attention. The costs that actually decide whether a product works are elsewhere, and no
      subscription changes them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/guides/landed-cost-fee-stack/">What a unit actually costs to land</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/amazon-fba-fee-changes-2026/">The 2026 fee changes</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/fba-reimbursement-sourcing-cost/">What a lost unit is actually worth</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="btn-row" style="margin:2rem 0 3rem">
    <Btn href="/tools/margin-calculator/">Run your own numbers</Btn>
    <Btn href="/how-we-test/" quiet={true}>Read the rubric</Btn>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
